\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Why are LMs boring? Because LMs have been trained on vast corpora of human writing that reflect the norms of everyday communication, which typically prioritize clarity and informativeness over novelty and variation. While LMs such as GPT-2 \citep{radford2019language}, RoBERTa, and BERT have achieved remarkable training and evaluation results, these models are increasingly being deployed by non-researchers outside of controlled experiments. In these real-world settings, users may have intentions or goals that differ from standard writing clarity. For instance, teachers might want to confuse students, doctors might aim to bore patients, and designers might seek to prevent users from clicking away. Consequently, the text generated by LMs in these scenarios might fail to meet the specific intentions of the users relying on them.

