\begin{abstract}
This paper presents a simple and robust method to automatically classify white blood cells into their specific types. This task is typically accomplished using deep neural networks, particularly CNNs, which have achieved state-of-the-art performance, especially with pooling CNNs that leverage spatial connectivity between neurons. We can improve performance by incorporating spatial connectivity data into a deep CNN model. The proposed solution involves using a stochastic gradient descent-trained CNN on nuclei images to extract features for a probabilistic neural network (PNN) to predict cell types using these features. Experimental results on both simulated and real data show the proposed approach performs comparable to state-of-the-art approaches. The proposed model achieves a neutrophil segmentation accuracy of 98.9\% and a classification accuracy of 99.0\% for six different cell types in simulated data.
